  <h2 id="pixel-count">The Pixel Count: 4x the Detail</h2>
  <p>The primary difference between 1080p (Full HD) and 4K (Ultra HD) is the sheer number of pixels on the screen.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>1080p</strong> has a resolution of 1920x1080, which equals roughly <strong>2 million pixels</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>4K</strong> has a resolution of 3840x2160, which equals roughly <strong>8.3 million pixels</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>That means a 4K image contains four times the visual data of a 1080p image. On a large TV or a high-end monitor, a 4K video will look significantly sharper, revealing fine details (like individual hairs or blades of grass) that 1080p blurs out.</p>

  <h2 id="file-size">The Trade-off: Massive File Sizes</h2>
  <p>Because 4K contains four times the data, the files are significantly larger. Even when using highly efficient codecs like H.265 (HEVC), a 4K video download can easily be 3x to 5x larger than the 1080p equivalent.</p>
  <p>If you have limited storage space on your phone or hard drive, downloading a playlist of 4K videos will fill it up incredibly fast.</p>

  <h2 id="hardware">Hardware Requirements</h2>
  <p>Decoding 8.3 million pixels 30 times a second requires significant processing power. While almost any smartphone from the last decade can play a 1080p video smoothly without draining the battery, playing a high-bitrate 4K video on an older device can lead to stuttering, overheating, and rapid battery drain.</p>

